# clustering.in - CPPTRAJ
#
# Clusters the ligand binding pose over the production runs and writes one
# representative structure per cluster.
#
# Usage:
#   cpptraj -i clustering.in
# Run from a FRESH directory: output names are fixed, so running twice in the
# same folder silently overwrites the previous results, including the rep.c*.pdb
# files used for figures.
#
# Replace before running:
#   SYSTEM  -> system name         (e.g. BRSV_L_isoquercetin)
#   LIG     -> ligand resname      (e.g. TIL)
#   POCKET  -> binding-pocket residue list, comma-separated
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Conventions and the reasoning behind them:
#
#   The three replicates are concatenated so that clusters are defined on a
#   common basis. Without this, cluster 0 of one replicate has no relation to
#   cluster 0 of another and the states cannot be compared.
#
#   Superposition uses the backbone of the POCKET, not of the whole protein.
#   This matters: with a 2,161-residue protein, global backbone alignment is
#   dominated by regions far from the site, and small global differences show up
#   as apparent ligand displacement. Clustering under global alignment separated
#   the trajectories by replicate rather than by pose (SSR/SST 0.55) and missed a
#   transition that the native-contact analysis had detected; with pocket
#   alignment the same transition appeared and SSR/SST rose to 0.91.
#
#   The clustering metric is the LIGAND alone, heavy atoms, no re-fitting. The
#   preceding rms sets the frame of reference; nofit means the RMSD measures
#   where the ligand sits within that reference.
#
#   epsilon 1.5 A. Chosen because it resolves the conformational transition
#   independently identified by the native-contact analysis; a larger cutoff
#   merges the states on either side of it. Keep the same value across all
#   systems, or cluster counts are not comparable between them.
#
#   sieve 10 builds the pairwise matrix from every tenth frame and assigns the
#   rest afterwards. Restoration is automatic - "sieverestore" is not a valid
#   keyword. Raise to 20 if memory is tight.
#
#   Frame 501 onward discards the first 5 ns of each run, matching the
#   equilibration criterion used elsewhere.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Outputs:
#   cnumvtime.dat   cluster assigned to each frame (13,500 rows: 4,500 per
#                   replicate, in the order the trajectories were loaded)
#   summary.dat     size, average distance and centroid frame of each cluster
#   info.dat        cluster count, DBI, pSF and SSR/SST
#   cpopvtime.dat   population of each cluster over time, pooled
#   rep.c*.pdb      representative structure of each cluster
#
# Sanity checks on the results:
#   - clusters shared between replicates: if every cluster belongs to a single
#     replicate, the superposition is probably still removing the wrong motion
#   - SSR/SST: below ~0.7 the clusters explain little of the variance
#   - cluster boundaries should line up with events seen in other analyses

parm SYSTEM_solvated_dry.prmtop

trajin SYSTEM_solvated_prod_dry.nc 501 last
trajin SYSTEM_solvated_prod_rep2_dry.nc 501 last
trajin SYSTEM_solvated_prod_rep3_dry.nc 501 last

autoimage

rms fitpocket :POCKET@CA,N,C first mass

cluster C1 \
  hieragglo epsilon 1.5 averagelinkage \
  rms :LIG&!@H= nofit mass \
  sieve 10 \
  out cnumvtime.dat \
  summary summary.dat \
  info info.dat \
  repout rep repfmt pdb \
  cpopvtime cpopvtime.dat normframe

go
quit
